<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kazikumukh Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1642 CE – 1860 CE · capital Kumukh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Lak khanate in the mountains of central Dagestan whose ruler Surkhay Khan briefly controlled Shirvan in the 1720s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the shamkhals moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarki">Tarki</a> around 1642, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lak">Lak</a> heartland at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumukh">Kumukh</a> became a separate state under its own rulers, who took the title khan. The khanate kept the prestige of Kumukh as an old centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lak">Lak</a> villages known for craftsmen, traders and seasonal workers, and drew wealth from raids and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> service. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surkhay-khan-i">Surkhay Khan I</a> joined the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajji-davud">Hajji Davud</a> in sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> in 1721, an event that helped trigger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great's</a> Persian campaign, and briefly ruled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvan</a> as an Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> later defeated him.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The khanate accepted Russian protection in 1820. Its khans, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aslan-khan-of-kazikumukh">Aslan Khan</a>, sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/caucasian-imamate">Caucasian Imamate</a>, and in 1860 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> abolished it. Its lands became the Kazikumukh district of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan-oblast">Dagestan Oblast</a>, the heart of today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lak">Lak</a> region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kazikumukh-khanate.jpg" alt="Kazikumukh Khanate" width="320" height="187"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gazikumukhhanflag.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1642 CE – 1860 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kumukh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Surkhay Khan I, Aslan Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lak</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gazikumukh Khanate, Lak Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1751&amp;focus=kazikumukh-khanate" aria-label="Kazikumukh Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5824 677 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kazikumukh Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6144 933L6144 933M6144 933L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6144 933h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6144 933h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6144 933h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kumukh. <a href="/atlas?year=1751&amp;focus=kazikumukh-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
